\section{Discussion and Future Directions}
\label{sec:discussion}

The occupancy auxiliary head improves collision avoidance by reshaping the vision encoder's internal representations at zero inference cost. This implicit approach is effective across the SafeLIBERO benchmark, yet it does not provide formal safety guarantees. In dense clutter scenarios where multiple objects are tightly packed, implicit spatial awareness may be insufficient to prevent all collisions, because the policy must rely on the encoder having learned sufficiently fine-grained spatial boundaries for every possible configuration.

A natural extension is to construct an explicit safety layer that conditions on the spatial features already learned by the occupancy-trained encoder. We term this direction \textbf{OCC-CBF} (Occupancy-Conditioned Control Barrier Function): a learned barrier function $h(z_{\text{occ}}, x_{\text{robot}}) \geq 0$ that filters the VLA's proposed actions to maintain a safety invariant, where $z_{\text{occ}}$ denotes the encoder's spatial features and $x_{\text{robot}}$ the robot state. Unlike existing barrier function approaches that rely on hand-crafted geometric representations (Euclidean distances in AEGIS~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, discrete occupancy grids in OGM-CBF~\cite{raja2024ogmcbf}, or object-centric decompositions in DCBF~\cite{kwon2026learningnudge}), OCC-CBF would operate directly in the VLA's continuous learned feature space. This avoids the need for explicit object segmentation or distance computation at test time and allows the barrier to reason about spatial relationships that the encoder has already learned to represent. We leave the formulation, training, and empirical validation of OCC-CBF to future work.

\section{Experiments}

\subsection{Benchmark and Tasks}

We evaluate on the SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, which augments the LIBERO-Spatial suite with collision-aware evaluation. SafeLIBERO comprises 32 scenarios across 10 tabletop manipulation tasks organized into two difficulty levels: Level~I (basic spatial reasoning) and Level~II (complex multi-step manipulation). Each task requires the agent to reach, grasp, and place objects among surrounding obstacles. Several tasks deliberately position objects in close proximity (e.g., a bowl resting on a ramekin), creating natural collision challenges that test whether the agent can complete the instructed task without disturbing nearby objects.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}

We measure two complementary metrics aligned with the VLSA-AEGIS protocol~\cite{hu2025vlsa}. \textbf{Task Success Rate (TSR)} is the fraction of episodes in which the robot completes the instructed task. \textbf{Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR)} is the fraction of episodes with zero unintended object displacement, computed as $\text{CAR} = 1 - n_{\text{collide}} / N$, where $n_{\text{collide}}$ counts episodes whose maximum L1 \texttt{body\_xpos} displacement exceeds a threshold of $0.001$. For each of the 10 tasks, we run 20 rollouts per random seed across 3 seeds (42, 43, 44), yielding 60 rollouts per task and 600 total. We report mean and 95\% confidence intervals, with paired $t$-tests and Cohen's $d$ effect sizes for pairwise comparisons.

\subsection{Experimental Conditions}

We design five conditions that form a mechanism ablation chain, progressively isolating the role of occupancy-grounded gradient flow:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
  \item \textbf{Vanilla}: OpenVLA-OFT fine-tuned without any auxiliary supervision (baseline).
  \item \textbf{+Occ}: BEV occupancy auxiliary head with gradients flowing into the vision encoder (\texttt{occ\_detach=False}).
  \item \textbf{+Occ-Detach}: Occupancy head with detached gradients (\texttt{occ\_detach=True}), testing whether gradient flow to the encoder is necessary.
  \item \textbf{+Depth}: Depth prediction auxiliary head (same architecture, labels from Depth Anything V2), testing whether collision avoidance is specific to occupancy or arises from any geometric auxiliary task.
  \item \textbf{+Random-Occ}: Occupancy head trained with randomized labels, controlling for the regularization effect of an auxiliary loss.
\end{itemize}

\noindent The chain Vanilla $\to$ +Random-Occ $\to$ +Depth $\to$ +Occ-Detach $\to$ +Occ isolates, in turn, auxiliary regularization, geometric supervision, and occupancy-specific gradient flow.

\subsection{Implementation Details}

All conditions share the same base model: OpenVLA-OFT 7B~\cite{kim2025oft}, pre-trained on Open X-Embodiment. We fine-tune with LoRA (rank 32, $\alpha{=}16$) for 50K steps using AdamW (weight decay $0.01$), a batch size of 8, a constant learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-4}$, and 3 random seeds per condition. The LoRA adapter adds 110.8M trainable parameters (1.45\% of 7.65B total).

The occupancy head (1.2M parameters) is a convolutional decoder that maps concatenated DINOv2+SigLIP patch features (2176 dimensions) to a $64{\times}64$ BEV occupancy grid. The first 256 patch tokens from the primary camera view are selected and projected via a linear layer ($2176{\to}256$), then reshaped into a $16{\times}16$ spatial map. Two transposed convolutions ($256{\to}128{\to}64$, kernel 4, stride 2) with BatchNorm and GELU upsample this map to $64{\times}64$, and a $1{\times}1$ convolution produces the binary occupancy logits. The head is trained with Focal Loss ($\alpha{=}0.25$, $\gamma{=}2.0$); the occupancy loss weight $\lambda_{\text{occ}}$ starts at $0.01$ and decays exponentially as $\lambda_{\text{occ}}(t) = 0.01 \cdot \exp(-t/\tau)$ with $\tau{=}50$K steps. The depth head uses the same decoder architecture with continuous regression targets. Each run trains in approximately 12.9 hours on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPU (98\,GB). Evaluation uses \texttt{MUJOCO\_GL=egl} for headless rendering with deterministic rollout seeds.

